00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:14.390
is misinformation here to stay?

00:01:14.390 --> 00:01:16.590
So what is misinformation anyway?

00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:19.650
Well as defined by the CUNY
Graduate School of Journalism,

00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:23.190
it's the sharing of inaccurate
and misleading information

00:01:23.190 --> 00:01:24.720
in an unintentional way.

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:25.710
It's often accompanied

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:28.770
by its more villainous
sibling, disinformation,

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:32.100
which is misinformation spread
with the intention to deceive

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:34.140
typically to further political goals.

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:36.300
Now, miss and disinformation

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:38.160
have been around since
the first Neanderthal

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:40.230
took false credit for inventor fire,

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:43.800
but misinformation needs
widespread mediums of communication

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:44.880
to really spread.

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:46.080
Take ancient Rome

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:47.130
where coins were basically

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:49.470
the first mass produced
methods of communication,

00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:51.990
regularly used to project ruler's power

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:53.610
and convey state propaganda.

00:01:53.610 --> 00:01:54.570
It didn't take long

00:01:54.570 --> 00:01:57.210
for Julius Caesar's adopted son, Octavian,

00:01:57.210 --> 00:01:58.890
to use engraved coins

00:01:58.890 --> 00:02:01.620
to spread disinformation
about his rival, Mark Antony,

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:03.120
calling him among other things,

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:06.900
a raging alcoholic and a
trader for marrying Cleopatra.

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:09.360
Skip ahead to the invention
of the printing press,

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:11.640
which significantly lowered the barrier

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:13.170
to circulate your ideas.

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:15.450
There were political
ramifications to this,

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:16.950
like when rebels trying to take down

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:18.870
England's King George II,

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:20.760
filled papers with fake stories

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:22.230
claiming that he was ill.

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:24.150
King George ultimately prevailed,

00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:26.070
but the rebels kick-started a solid game

00:02:26.070 --> 00:02:27.300
of political health shaming

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:29.160
that, unlike King George,

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:31.200
remains alive and well today.

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:34.320
As for America, it's been
a wash with misinformation

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:35.550
since the colonial days,

00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:36.930
when posters and pamphlets

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:39.420
propagated biased politicized news.

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:42.630
Benjamin Franklin himself
amplified revolutionary fervor

00:02:42.630 --> 00:02:44.010
by pending propaganda stories

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:47.580
about Indigenous Americans
colluding with King George III.

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:48.990
I don't know much for sure,

00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:51.030
but I know that if your
name is King George

00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:52.590
people probably hate you.

00:02:52.590 --> 00:02:55.770
When American newspapers
emerged in the early 18OOs,

00:02:55.770 --> 00:02:58.890
they balanced hard news with
straight up fake stories,

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:00.900
like the "Great Moon Hoax"

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:02.250
in which the "New York Sun" claimed

00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:04.890
there was an alien colony on the moon.

00:03:04.890 --> 00:03:08.160
By the 1890s, what we now
call yellow journalism

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:10.050
erupted as newspaper magnates.

00:03:10.050 --> 00:03:12.450
Joseph Pulitzer and
William Randolph Hearst

00:03:12.450 --> 00:03:14.160
battled in a circulation war

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:17.190
using misleading and fantastical
headlines to sell papers.

00:03:17.190 --> 00:03:19.350
Think of it as early click bait,

00:03:19.350 --> 00:03:21.570
except they weren't clicking,
they were grabbing papers.

00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:22.650
So we'll call it grab bait.

00:03:22.650 --> 00:03:24.120
The influence of yellow journalism

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:26.430
is often cited as one
of the driving forces

00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:27.960
of the Spanish-American War.

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:31.680
So yes, some guys that own
newspapers helped start a war.

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:33.030
It sounds fun.

00:03:33.030 --> 00:03:36.180
Unsurprisingly, this is
around when the term fake news

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:37.770
seems to have enter the lexicon.

00:03:37.770 --> 00:03:42.060
If it sounds like early America
was a wash in (bleep) news,

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:43.770
it's because it totally was.

00:03:43.770 --> 00:03:46.080
But as philosopher, Jacob Soll, writes,

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:48.840
yellow journalism had a backlash effect.

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:52.260
As people sought out more
objective or reputable news,

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:53.580
newspapers started hiring

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:55.380
better-educated reporters and journalists

00:03:55.380 --> 00:03:58.500
to cover fact-based news
about business in the economy

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:00.360
for the wealthy investor class.

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:02.820
Papers started relying
on the subscription model

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:05.940
which further disincentivized
disinformation

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:08.010
because customers could simply switch

00:04:08.010 --> 00:04:09.330
to a competitor's paper

00:04:09.330 --> 00:04:11.010
if they felt they were being duped.

00:04:11.010 --> 00:04:14.100
So again, the free
market fixes everything.

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:16.950
All of this created an
entirely novel media climate.

00:04:16.950 --> 00:04:18.637
As historian Jonathan Ladd writes,

00:04:18.637 --> 00:04:20.970
"The existence of an
independent, powerful,

00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:23.280
widely respected news media establishment

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:25.440
is an historical anomaly.

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:26.670
Prior to the 20th century,

00:04:26.670 --> 00:04:29.940
such an institution had never
existed in American history."

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:32.490
The government also
played a big role in this.

00:04:32.490 --> 00:04:36.330
By 1937, a Roper poll
asking, is the press fair

00:04:36.330 --> 00:04:39.570
showed that 66% of Americans said yes.

00:04:39.570 --> 00:04:41.610
Continuing this trend in 1949

00:04:41.610 --> 00:04:43.410
after television entered the picture,

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:45.090
the Federal Communications Commission

00:04:45.090 --> 00:04:47.010
sought to preserve objective news

00:04:47.010 --> 00:04:48.480
with the fairness doctrine,

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:52.080
which required broadcast news
to present balanced coverage

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:54.810
and to supplement controversial
opinions with counterpoints.

00:04:54.810 --> 00:04:59.400
When the policy was axed by
Ronald Reagan's FCC in 1987,

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:00.390
everything changed.

00:05:00.390 --> 00:05:02.670
Wow, Ronald Reagan ruined
something in the 80s.

00:05:02.670 --> 00:05:03.630
This is crazy.

00:05:03.630 --> 00:05:05.400
According to the "Washington Post,"

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:08.820
almost overnight, the media
landscape was transformed.

00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:11.100
Opinionated news,
including talk radio shows

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:12.120
like Rush Limbaugh's

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:13.770
exploded on the scene.

