to the spread of misinformation.

00:10:47.220 --> 00:10:48.840
We talked about this in a recent video,

00:10:48.840 --> 00:10:49.800
but it's worth repeating.

00:10:49.800 --> 00:10:53.610
Controversial ideas equate
to virality on social media.

00:10:53.610 --> 00:10:56.647
As journalist, Stuart A Thompson
and Charlie Warzel write,

00:10:56.647 --> 00:10:59.310
"Facebook's algorithms
have coaxed many people

00:10:59.310 --> 00:11:01.890
into sharing more extreme
views on the platform,

00:11:01.890 --> 00:11:04.230
rewarding them with likes and shares

00:11:04.230 --> 00:11:07.515
for posts on subjects like
election fraud conspiracies,

00:11:07.515 --> 00:11:11.190
COVID=19 denialism, and
anti-vaccination rhetoric."

00:11:11.190 --> 00:11:12.300
In recent years,

00:11:12.300 --> 00:11:15.780
such stories of an amplified
by bot farms and meme accounts,

00:11:15.780 --> 00:11:17.940
which efficiently churn out misinformation

00:11:17.940 --> 00:11:21.180
in the form of recognizable
visual jokes and catchy slogans.

00:11:21.180 --> 00:11:24.570
Importantly, memes are
extremely hard to moderate,

00:11:24.570 --> 00:11:27.000
or if you're a member of Gen X,

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:28.620
extremely hard to understand.

00:11:28.620 --> 00:11:30.570
So even when social media platforms

00:11:30.570 --> 00:11:32.670
attempt to weed out disinformation,

00:11:32.670 --> 00:11:37.020
images of Obama as a lizard
person can skirt by undetected.

00:11:37.020 --> 00:11:39.030
But while it's easy to
blame misinformation

00:11:39.030 --> 00:11:41.640
on the evolved media landscape,

00:11:41.640 --> 00:11:43.830
we think there's something more going on.

00:11:43.830 --> 00:11:47.070
Harsin argues that we live
in a cultural ambiance

00:11:47.070 --> 00:11:51.480
of post-truth defined by a
breakdown of social trust.

00:11:51.480 --> 00:11:52.890
Harsin blames the mainstreaming

00:11:52.890 --> 00:11:54.870
of this public anxiety about truth

00:11:54.870 --> 00:11:57.390
on the influence and
persuasion industries,

00:11:57.390 --> 00:12:00.870
like PR, marketing, and
political communications.

00:12:00.870 --> 00:12:03.960
He writes that they went from
appealing to people's reason

00:12:03.960 --> 00:12:06.840
to strategically managing
their emotions and attention

00:12:06.840 --> 00:12:09.630
via organized systematic lying.

00:12:09.630 --> 00:12:12.810
Such lying is now seen
as banal, unremarkable,

00:12:12.810 --> 00:12:15.780
even expected in commercials
or political speech.

00:12:15.780 --> 00:12:17.340
Harsin argues that increasingly,

00:12:17.340 --> 00:12:19.527
journalism and opinionated commentary

00:12:19.527 --> 00:12:21.420
use the same emotional appeals

00:12:21.420 --> 00:12:23.280
of politicians and advertisers.

00:12:23.280 --> 00:12:25.890
As a result, people tend
to measure believability

00:12:25.890 --> 00:12:27.990
in what makes them feel the most,

00:12:27.990 --> 00:12:32.310
and outrageous disinformation
can make us feel a lot.

00:12:32.310 --> 00:12:34.800
It's fitting that Alex
Jones's reporting style

00:12:34.800 --> 00:12:36.870
is defined by his pathos.

00:12:36.870 --> 00:12:40.110
Frequently expressed an angry
and passionate outburst.

00:12:40.110 --> 00:12:41.250
In the absence of evidence,

00:12:41.250 --> 00:12:44.526
the proof lies in his emotional appeal

00:12:44.526 --> 00:12:47.370
- Infowars.com-
- It's dawn half past 11.

